## Consequences

Positive consequences:

- negative supervision remains traceable after rasterization
- ordinary and hard-negative evidence can be analysed separately
- overlap is not double-counted in unique negative totals
- later sampling policies can use source provenance without rereading semantic information from label values
- false-positive analysis can distinguish which negative evidence was available during training

Trade-offs:

- provenance requires additional source-specific rasterization
- the provenance artifact stores records for the complete candidate population
- private reference sources remain required to reproduce the masks from first principles

## Alternatives Considered

### Infer Provenance from Label Value Zero

Rejected because the numeric label merges both negative-source semantics.

### Store Only One Provenance Class per Tile

Rejected because a tile may contain both ordinary and hard-negative evidence and may contain overlapping source pixels.

### Preserve Only Selected-Tile Provenance

Rejected because complete candidate coverage is required to audit selection and support future policy changes.

### Treat Overlap as Two Negative Pixels

Rejected because one raster location must contribute one unique negative training target.

